{
	title: 'Markdown in model text; a streaming block never shrinks',
	spec: `Model text (text and thinking blocks, not tool output; the user's own prompts stay as typed) is rendered as markdown in both clients, from one parser in src/common so the terminal and the web never disagree. Supported: fenced code blocks (content kept exactly as written; fence markers never shown, their terminal rows kept blank; the language shown quietly above), tables, # headings (markers hidden, the heading bold, as old Hal), lists, > quotes, and inline **bold**, *italic*, _italic_, \`code\` and [links](url) (http, https, and host-relative /<session id>#<block id> addresses only; the label shown bold, the address hidden in the terminal as an OSC 8 target, a real <a> on the web, as old Hal). A few HTML entities are decoded outside code (&nbsp; &amp; &lt; &gt; &quot;), so a line holding only &nbsp;, which models write to force a blank line, becomes a blank row. Hal is monospace everywhere, so code (inline spans and blocks) is set apart by colour alone: the item style's code colour (colors.ts), as SGR in the terminal and var(--code) on the web. The terminal draws styles with SGR and aligns table columns, wrapping cells inside their columns as old Hal does, so a table is always visible whole; the web builds real elements (a <table> for tables, cells wrapping; a table that cannot fit even so, typical on a phone, scrolls sideways inside its own box, with a visible cue at the clipped edge, such as a fade, so the user knows there is more), and model text never reaches the page as HTML: everything is escaped before markup, so a reply containing <script> or <img onerror> shows as text. Budget: about 300 non-test lines for parser and both renderers. Old Hal is the reference (artifacts old-markdown.ts, old-md.ts, old-web-markdown.ts and their tests); copy its behaviour, not its structure.

A streaming block never shrinks, only grows (the user saw the web scroll position tremble as a streaming table grew and shrank). Two parts. (1) The parser is kind to prefixes: a trailing line that could start or continue a table (it begins with |) is held back until the next line arrives, a table is drawn only once its separator row has arrived, and every construct opens optimistically and is closed at the end of the input: an opening ** or * or \` hides its marker and styles what follows at once, an open \`\`\` fence is a code block to the end. A guess that turns out wrong (a paragraph ending with no closing marker) shows the marker again, which only adds width. A * or ** opens only after the start of a line, a space or punctuation and before a non-space (so 2**3 and a*b never open); _ never opens or closes inside a word (snake_case); [ opens a link before a non-space and shows again if ]( does not follow; a # run at the start of a line waits for the next character to know if it is a heading; one at the very end of the input, whose next character is not known yet, is held back until it is. Inline styles never run past the end of their paragraph or cell. (2) A safety net that guarantees the rule whatever the renderer does: each client keeps, for the one block that is streaming, the tallest height it has drawn it at, and pads it to that height (terminal: blank rows at its end; web: min-height on the card). The high-water mark belongs to that block only, lives in memory, starts from the first render of the block (also after a reconnect or restart), and is kept after the block stops streaming, since a late shrink is the very jolt to avoid (a block slowly growing to 140 px, then snapping to 120): the terminal keeps the padding until its next full redraw (a width change, a tab switch, Ctrl-L), the web keeps the min-height within the same layout, resetting the obsolete floor when its available width or font/line-height changes (and starting a new peak if still streaming). Rotation must not retain a portrait-height blank tail behind rewrapped landscape text. It is never derived from other blocks or tabs and never persisted (a high-water mark of painted height, peak, once put the terminal prompt mid-screen after a restart; see task vq).`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Old web markdown (old-web-markdown.ts) builds an HTML string with markdown.escape first and sets innerHTML; its /status usage-table special case (usageCell, meters) is not wanted here. Either escape-then-markup or DOM building is fine; the test is that <script>, <img onerror> and a javascript: link in model text stay inert.',
		"Where it plugs in: terminal model text is drawn in src/client/item-view.ts (case 'text') via frame.itemRows; the Hal cursor (task 5p) is appended to the last streamed row by withCursor in frame.ts, so the renderer must keep the last row's visible width known. Web model text is src/web/components/Card.tsx. The streaming block is the one 5p calls streaming (t.live, running, last item is the live turn's last text block).",
		'Why shrinking happens, so the tests aim right: a | line shown as plain text wraps differently once it becomes a table cell; the separator row appears or disappears; a new wide row widens a column and earlier rows rewrap into fewer rows (web table layout does this by itself); closing ** removes 4 visible chars. Test the rule by feeding one reply a character at a time and checking each frame is no shorter than the one before (terminal rows; web: the card min-height), for a reply with a table, a code fence and bold text.',
		"Data (28 Sep, all 22,993 finished assistant texts in old Hal and hal2 sessions on this machine, markers outside code): ** 46,625 pairs, 2 never closed (both model slips: 'at **17:40:06', '**80% of B'); * 1,606 pairs, 3 never closed; the char before a real * or ** opener was never a letter or digit (space 90%, line start 10%, rest punctuation), hence the rule. _ 5 pairs vs 386 inert (snake_case): _italic_ is nearly unused. Backtick spans 82,084, 22 unclosed (mostly models writing about backticks). Fences 6,665, 2 unclosed (a paused reply). [ 1,049 links vs 172 not ([A1], [x], [ ] checkboxes, [...]). Optimistic opening would almost never be wrong.",
		'Shape: src/common/markdown.ts parses into blocks (line with kind p/h/li/quote, code, table) of styled runs {text, bold, italic, code, href}, never HTML. client/markdown-view.ts draws them as SGR rows (each row closes what it opens: plain text is wrapped with strings.wordWrap, then each row takes the styles of the runs its offsets came from); web/components/Markdown.tsx builds elements from them with non-keyed For/Match, so a delta updates in place. Bare URLs (task hb) go where scan() in markdown.ts marks it.',
		"Streaming is a parse flag (terminal: the item has the stream cursor, frame.itemRows' hal; web: Card's cursor prop). Only then is anything held back or opened optimistically; done text holds nothing back and shows an unclosed marker as written, so the end of a stream only grows it.",
		'Held-back lines are blank rows, not missing rows: dropping them made the count fall when a trailing empty line (text ending in \\n) turned into a | line. Likewise a lone complete | row followed only by an empty tail line waits (its separator may be next). The tail line also drops trailing * _ [ \\ and a partial &entity, which the next character decides. With these, the terminal renderer alone never lost a row streaming the test reply a character at a time at 24, 40 and 80 columns; the high-water mark is the net for the rest.',
		'Terminal high-water mark: frame.state.peaks, keyed by cols, session and item key, applied in frame.build (frame.highWater); render.draw(true) and render.reset() clear it. Tests sharing a session id and key across cases rely on render.reset() clearing it (hal-cursor.test.ts failed before that).',
		"Web high-water mark is min-height on the .Markdown root, not the card: a min-height on a folding thinking card would stop it folding. Hal's cursor is Markdown's last child (children prop) and the last line turns inline via .line:has(+ .cursor), so the cursor follows the text; the CDP test looks for it there. Web table layout does shrink a streaming table (the CDP check fails without the min-height), so the net is load-bearing on the web.",
		'The sideways cue for a wide table is a mask fading its right edge, driven by a scroll timeline on a registered --fade property (no JS); where animation-timeline is unsupported (Firefox today) there is no cue but it still scrolls. The min-height stays after a resize (checked: phone-width peak left a tall card at desktop width), as the spec asks.',
		'Checked by hand via CDP screenshots at 390 and 1000 px: headings, lists, quote bar, bold link, escaped <script>/<img> text, table with fade, code block with its language.',
		'Task v8 adds safe host-relative session links to Markdown: /<session id> with an optional #<numeric block id> or #<n>.<i>, in addition to http(s) links. These are needed for /find results and remain relative on the web; other protocols (javascript:, file:, etc.) are not recognized. Terminal markdown-view resolves host-relative hrefs through ansi.webUrl, preserving the one-time-code link contract of e3.',
		'Task 3y supersedes the earlier note that a phone-width peak stays at desktop width: the high-water mark remains after streaming ends only within the same width/font layout. A native ResizeObserver clears obsolete floors on reflow, preventing a huge blank tail that makes bottom distances and reading anchors wrong.',
		'Fence markers were once drawn verbatim in the terminal, so users saw ``` around code. Invariant: rendered Markdown never shows fence markers. Their rows stay (blank, or the quiet language) so a streaming fence never loses a row.',
		'Code once looked like prose: both clients are monospace and neither used the theme code colour, so fences were the only cue. Invariant: code text is drawn in style.code.',
